<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tezpur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tezpur is a town on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Nearby lay Haruppeshvara, the capital of the Mlechchha dynasty of Kamarupa, and the area holds early remains such as the carved stone doorframe of Da Parbatia. Legend connects it with the story of Usha and Aniruddha and the demon king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabhatta">Bana</a>. It is now a district headquarters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tezpur.jpg" alt="Tezpur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anupom007bora at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agnigarh_Hill,_Tezpur.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tezpur lay under the Koch dynasty and in the sixteenth century became part of the Ahom kingdom, whose rulers are known for their administration and patronage of art and culture. The city saw several battles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. At Da Parbatia, a village close to the west of the city, excavations in 1924 uncovered a stone door frame with extensive carvings from a sixth-century temple, over which a brick <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple of the Ahom period was later built. A large artificial lake, the Harjara Pukhuri, was excavated in the early ninth century and is named after Harjara Varmana of the Mleccha dynasty, and the Bamuni Hills hold ruins with sculpture of the ninth and tenth centuries.</p><p>Local tradition ties many places to the legend of Banasura, a king of the asuras. His daughter Usha is said to have worshipped at the Bhairabi Temple, and the hillock called Agnigarh, the hill of fire, is held to be where she was kept, surrounded by fire, in her romance with Aniruddha, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. At the Rudrapada Temple on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> left the print of his left foot on a stone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tezpur stands on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> in Sonitpur district, 175 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>, and is the largest of the cities on the north bank. The population of the municipal area was 102,505 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is an educational centre, with Tezpur University and several colleges. At Bhomoraguri a stone inscription records the plan of the Ahom general Kalia Bhomora Borphukan to bridge the Brahmaputra; almost two centuries later a bridge of 3.015 km, named after him, joins Tezpur to Silghat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tezpur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After him the Mlechchha dynasty of Salastambha ruled from Haruppeshvara, near <b>Tezpur</b>, followed by the Pala dynasty of Kamarupa.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamarupa">Kamarupa</a></li></ul></section>
